Q: Define the standard $n$-simplex $\Delta^n$.
A: The convex hull of the standard basis $e_0, \dots, e_n$ in $\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$: all $\sum t_i e_i$ with $\sum t_i = 1$ and $t_i \geq 0$. The $t_i$ are barycentric coordinates.
---
C: The face inclusion $d^i : \Delta^{n-1} \to \Delta^n$ is the affine map sending vertices to vertices in order and [omitting the vertex $e_i$].
---
Q: What is a singular $n$-simplex in a space $X$?
A: A continuous map $\sigma : \Delta^n \to X$. The set of all of them is $\mathrm{Sin}_n(X)$.
---
C: The $i$-th face of a singular simplex $\sigma$ is $d_i \sigma = $ [$\sigma \circ d^i$|precompose], an element of $\mathrm{Sin}_{n-1}(X)$.
---
Q: Define the group $S_n(X)$ of singular $n$-chains.
A: The free abelian group on $\mathrm{Sin}_n(X)$: finite sums $\sum a_i \sigma_i$ with integer coefficients. For $n < 0$ it is $0$.
N: The free group exists so that $d_0 \sigma - d_1 \sigma$ makes sense for a loop $\sigma$.
---
Q: Write the boundary operator $d : S_n(X) \to S_{n-1}(X)$ on a simplex.
A: $d\sigma = \sum_{i=0}^{n} (-1)^i d_i \sigma$, extended additively.
---
C: An $n$-cycle is a chain $c$ with [$dc = 0$]; the cycles form $Z_n(X) = \ker d$.
---
C: An $n$-boundary is an element of $B_n(X) = $ [$\operatorname{im}(d : S_{n+1}(X) \to S_n(X))$|image of which map].
---
Q: Define the $n$-th singular homology group of $X$.
A: $H_n(X) = Z_n(X) / B_n(X) = \ker(d : S_n \to S_{n-1}) / \operatorname{im}(d : S_{n+1} \to S_n)$.
---
Q: Which single fact makes $B_n(X) \subseteq Z_n(X)$, so that homology is defined?
A: $d^2 = 0$: every boundary is a cycle. (Problem set 1.)
---
Q: Define a chain complex.
A: A graded abelian group $\{A_n\}_{n \in \mathbb{Z}}$ with homomorphisms $d : A_n \to A_{n-1}$ such that $d^2 = 0$.
---
Q: Why are $Z_n(X)$ and $B_n(X)$ free abelian, and why does that not make $H_n(X)$ free?
A: Both are subgroups of the free abelian group $S_n(X)$, and subgroups of free abelian groups are free. A quotient of free groups need not be free.
---
C: For the torus $T$, $H_1(T) \cong$ [$\mathbb{Z} \oplus \mathbb{Z}$], generated by the two coordinate loops.
---
Q: State $H_q(S^n)$ for $n > 0$.
A: $\mathbb{Z}$ for $q = 0$ and $q = n$, and $0$ otherwise.
N: For $n = 0$, $H_0(S^0) = \mathbb{Z} \oplus \mathbb{Z}$: two points.
---
C: The Grassmannian $\mathrm{Gr}_k(\mathbb{R}^n)$ is the quotient of the Stiefel manifold $V_k(\mathbb{R}^n)$ by [taking the span of a frame], and $\mathrm{Gr}_1(\mathbb{R}^n) = $ [$\mathbb{RP}^{n-1}$].
